We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} The strength of HS advantage evidence varies by model, not scale. We classify evidence into three tiers based on seed robustness, position independence, and sample size: \textbf{strong} (OT-7B: 5/5 seeds HB, $\Delta$Prec $+20.9{\pm}1.3$pp, no position confound, selectivity 18.3pp, $n{=}47$); \textbf{moderate} (R1-8B: precision-robust but FPR seed-fragile at 3/5 HB, $n{=}43$; QwQ-32B$^\dagger$: 5/5 HB, $+19.9{\pm}2.5$pp, negative lead time, $n{=}19$); and \textbf{qualified} (R1-32B$^\dagger$: 5/5 HB, $\Delta$Prec std${=}2.0$pp, but position-mediated with residualized gap NS, $W{=}1$ only, $n{=}20$).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Guidance for future research.}
Robustness splits by model, not scale: OT-7B (strong) and QwQ-32B (moderate) are most stable at 5/5 seeds HB, R1-8B FPR is seed-fragile (moderate, 3/5 HB), and R1-32B (qualified) passes all seeds but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}). Distillation and native reasoning training produce qualitatively different safety representation geometry within the same architecture. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage) provide converging evidence of genuine safety representations. Our probing approach establishes correlational evidence; causal validation via distributed alignment search~\citep{geiger2025causal}, LEACE~\citep{belrose2023leace}, or inference-time intervention~\citep{li2024iti} is necessary to confirm that probed directions are causally active in the model's safety reasoning rather than read-only features.

\paragraph{Layer selection audit protocol.}
Based on our findings, we recommend a five-step protocol for any probing study that reports threshold-dependent metrics: (1)~train probes across all layers; (2)~compute $\geq$3 metrics at each layer (at least one threshold-free and one threshold-dependent); (3)~check for metric disagreement between threshold-free and threshold-dependent selection; (4)~report sensitivity to layer choice by including results at the 2nd and 3rd best layers; (5)~compute cosine volatility (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}) as a diagnostic for crossing-rate bias at candidate layers. Steps 3--5 cost negligible additional compute and would have prevented the spurious scale-dependent conclusion in our initial analysis.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Our primary contribution is methodological: layer selection is a critical, underexamined researcher degree of freedom in the probing literature. A biased composite metric incorporating crossing rate systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, where representation compression inflates threshold crossings independently of discriminative power, masking the HS advantage entirely. This finding generalizes beyond safety probing to any study that evaluates threshold-dependent metrics across transformer layers. The five-step audit protocol we propose (\cref{sec:discussion}) requires negligible additional compute and would have prevented the spurious scale-dependent conclusion in our initial analysis.

The empirical contribution is conditional on both encoder quality and model-specific factors. HS probes show a robust advantage at 7--8B (strong evidence for OT-7B, moderate for R1-8B) and a suggestive but position-mediated advantage at 32B (moderate for QwQ-32B, qualified for R1-32B with residualized gap NS). The advantage narrows from 4/4 models HB-significant against 384d MiniLM to 1/4 against 1024d BGE-large, establishing an encoder-quality boundary rather than an absolute modality difference. Current probes characterize the commitment phenomenon (FPR 39--52\%, oracle gap 85.5\% on R1-8B) rather than constitute production detectors; causal interventions~\citep{meng2022rome,li2024iti} and LLM-based text encoders~\citep{wang2024e5mistral} are the primary next steps toward closing this gap.

